$$
\prod_{z^{13} = 1} (z - (1 + i))(z - (1 - i)) = \left(\prod_{z^{13} = 1} (z - (1 + i))\right) \cdot \left(\prod_{z^{13} = 1} (z - (1 - i))\right)
$$

---

### Step 3: Use the polynomial identity

The polynomial whose roots are all the 13th roots of unity is:
$$
P(x) = x^{13} - 1
$$
So for any complex number $a$, we have:
$$
\prod_{z^{13} = 1} (z - a) = -P(a) = -(a^{13} - 1)
$$

Applying this, we get:
$$
\prod_{z^{13} = 1} (z - (1 + i)) = -( (1 + i)^{13} - 1 ), \quad
\prod_{z^{13} = 1} (z - (1 - i)) = -( (1 - i)^{13} - 1 )
$$

Thus, the full product is:
$$
\left[ -((1 + i)^{13} - 1) \right] \cdot \left[ -((1 - i)^{13} - 1) \right] = ((1 + i)^{13} - 1)((1 - i)^{13} - 1)
$$

